        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Rumi</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1207 CE – 1273 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">30 September 1207, to Persian parents at Wakhsh, a village in present-day Tajikistan; son of the preacher and jurist Bahā ud-Dīn Walad, of a family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a> preachers, and Mu'mina Khātūn</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">17 December 1273, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> in the Seljuk Sultanate of Rûm, after falling ill that month; buried beside his father</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Head of his father's madrassa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> from the age of twenty-five; Islamic jurist, preacher and teacher; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> master and poet</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masnavi">Masnavi</a>, a six-volume poem of about 27,000 lines; the Divan of Shams-e Tabrizi; his bond with the dervish Shams-e Tabrizi; teaching music, poetry and dance as a path to God; the Mevlevi Order of the Whirling Dervishes; the prose Fihi Ma Fihi</span></div>
