    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Rumi was trained as an Islamic scholar and succeeded his father as head of a madrassa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, where his family had settled after fleeing west before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>.</li><li>His meeting with the wandering dervish Shams-e Tabrizi in 1244, and Shams's disappearance in 1248, turned the jurist into an ascetic and a poet.</li><li>He wrote in Persian the Divan and the six books of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masnavi">Masnavi</a>, and after his death his followers and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Walad founded the Mevlevi Order.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Rumi wrote in Persian, his mother tongue, but his Divan also holds 90 ghazals and 19 quatrains in Arabic, a couple of dozen or so couplets in Turkish and 14 couplets in Greek, the Greek all in three poems that mix Greek and Persian.</p></aside>
    </section>

