    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1207</span><span class="rf-text">Rumi is born at Wakhsh, where his father, Bahā ud-Dīn Walad, is a preacher and jurist</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1212</span><span class="rf-text">The family moves to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> when he is aged around five</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1225</span><span class="rf-text">He marries Gowhar Khatun at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaman">Karaman</a>, where the family has settled on its way west</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1228</span><span class="rf-text">His father settles the family at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> in the Seljuk Sultanate of Rûm</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1244</span><span class="rf-text">He meets the dervish Shams-e Tabrizi, a meeting that changes him from a teacher and jurist into an ascetic</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1248</span><span class="rf-text">Shams is called to the back door one night and is never seen again</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1273</span><span class="rf-text">He dies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> and is buried beside his father; his followers found the Mevlevi Order</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sanai"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sanai.jpg" alt="Sana'i" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sanai">Sana'i</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%22Hakim_Sanai_addressing_the_Sultan_of_Ghazna%22_Folio_from_Hadiqat_al_Haqiqa_(The_Garden_of_Truth_and_The_Path_to_Trek)_MS_OR._1651,_fol._1b.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Rumi was born on 30 September 1207 to Persian parents at Wakhsh, a village in present-day Tajikistan. His father, Bahā ud-Dīn Walad, was a theologian, jurist and mystic who preached there and whom Rumi's followers later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">Ulama</a>, "Sultan of the Scholars"; his mother was Mu'mina Khātūn. For several generations the family had been preachers of the relatively liberal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a> Maturidi school. Later accounts made Rumi a descendant of Abu Bakr and, through his mother's line, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmshah">Khwarazmshah</a>; some modern scholars reject the first claim, and the second is seen as a hagiographical tradition meant to link the family with royalty.</p><p>The area was culturally adjacent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a>, then a major centre of Persian culture where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a> had developed for several centuries. Besides his father, who was connected to the spiritual lineage of Najm al-Din Kubra, the strongest influences on Rumi were the Persian poets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athtar">Attar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanai">Sanai</a>. The family lived at Wakhsh until 1212, when Rumi was about five, and then moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, sometime between 1215 and 1220, Bahā ud-Dīn set out westwards with his whole family and a group of disciples. A hagiographical account, not accepted by all Rumi scholars, has the poet Attar meet them at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a> and say of father and son, "Here comes a sea followed by an ocean." The caravan went on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, made the pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, and passed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and several Anatolian towns before settling for seven years at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaman">Karaman</a>, where Rumi's mother and brother died.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaman">Karaman</a> in 1225 Rumi married Gowhar Khatun, with whom he had two sons, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Walad and Ala-eddin Chalabi; after her death he married again and had another son and a daughter. On 1 May 1228, most likely on the insistent invitation of 'Alā' ud-Dīn Key-Qobād, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, his father settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, in the westernmost territories of the Seljuk Sultanate of Rûm. Anatolia had until recently belonged to the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> and was still known to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> and Turks as Rum, which is the source of the name Rumi.</p><p>Bahā ud-Dīn became head of a madrassa, and when he died Rumi, aged twenty-five, inherited his position. One of his father's students, Sayyed Burhan ud-Din Muhaqqiq Termazi, went on training him in the Shariah and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> path, and for nine years Rumi practised Sufism as his disciple, until Burhan ud-Din died in 1240 or 1241. His public life then began: he became an Islamic jurist, issued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwas</a>, gave sermons in the mosques of Konya and taught in the madrassa. In this period he also travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and is said to have spent four years there.</p><p>On 15 November 1244 he met the wandering dervish Shams-e Tabrizi, and the meeting changed his life: from an accomplished teacher and jurist he became an ascetic. Shams, the story goes, had travelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> praying for someone who could "endure my company", and a voice had named Jalal ud-Din of Konya. Sufi mysticism places great emphasis on the bond of master and disciple. Rumi and Shams became so absorbed in each other that Rumi neglected his duties.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/masnavi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/masnavi.jpg" alt="Masnavi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/masnavi">Masnavi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turkey.Konya049.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Georges Jansoone (JoJan), CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Rumi's poetry began with the loss of Shams in 1248. His love and his bereavement found expression in an outpouring of lyric poems, gathered as the Dīwān-e Kabīr or Dīwān-e Shams-e Tabrīzī, named in honour of his master. It holds approximately 35,000 Persian couplets and 2,000 Persian quatrains. His best-known work is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masnavi">Masnavi</a>, a poem in six books of approximately 27,000 lines that weaves fables, scenes from everyday life, Qur'anic revelation and exegesis, and metaphysics together. He dictated it over twelve years to his scribe and favourite student, Hussam-e Chalabi, who had suggested a book in the manner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanai">Sanai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athtar">Attar</a>; it opens, "Listen to the reed and the tale it tells, how it sings of separation". It has commonly been called "the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> in Persian".</p><p>His prose survives in three collections. Fihi Ma Fihi records seventy-one talks and lectures, compiled from his disciples' notes; the Majāles-e Sab'a holds seven sermons on the deeper meaning of Qur'an and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">Hadith</a>; and the Makatib gathers his letters to disciples, family members and men of state.</p><p>Like other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> poets of Persian literature, he speaks of a love that infuses the world. In his terms tawhid, the oneness of God, is lived most fully through love, which he calls "that flame which, when it blazes up, burns away everything except the Everlasting Beloved". He believed passionately in music, poetry and dance as a path for reaching God, and encouraged sama, listening to music and turning in the sacred dance; from these ideas the whirling of the dervishes developed into a ritual.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The gravest controversy of Rumi's life concerns Shams. On the night of 5 December 1248, as the two were talking, Shams was called to the back door, went out and was never seen again. There are many theories about his disappearance; the most popular are that Rumi's youngest son killed him, that he was killed for blasphemy, or that Shams, a known wanderer, simply chose to move on. For more than a month Rumi refused to believe the rumours of his death; after forty days he accepted it and began to dress in black.</p><p>The record of his life is itself disputed. The main biography, the Manāqib ul-Ārifīn of Shams ud-Din Ahmad Aflāki, written between 1318 and 1353, is hagiographical and mixes legend with fact; Franklin Lewis, author of the most complete modern biography, treats the hagiographical and the actual life in separate sections.</p><p>His religious outlook has been read in different ways. His work carries traces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-pluralism">religious pluralism</a>, as in the line "the lamps are different, but the Light is the same", yet he clearly holds the superiority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, calls Muhammad the most perfect of the prophets and criticises <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> for "overloading the image of God with superfluous structures and complications". Shibli Nomani argued that he should be seen as a serious theologian and not solely as a mystical poet. Edward G. Browne noted that Rumi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanai">Sanai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athtar">Attar</a> were all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslims; according to Annemarie Schimmel, the tendency of Shia authors to count Rumi among their own grew stronger after Twelver Shia became the state religion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> in 1501.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After Shams, Rumi found another companion in Salaḥ ud-Din-e Zarkub, a goldsmith, who served him for a decade. Zarkub was illiterate and pronounced some words incorrectly, and Rumi used some of those words in his poems as a sign of support and humility towards him. Rumi named him his successor, but Zarkub died first. Hussam-e Chalabi, his scribe, then took the companion's place. Walking with him one day in the Meram vineyards outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, Hussam proposed that Rumi write a long poem; Rumi is said to have taken out a paper bearing the opening eighteen lines of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masnavi">Masnavi</a>, and the dictation of its six volumes filled the next twelve years.</p><p>In December 1273 Rumi fell ill. He predicted his own death and composed a ghazal that begins, "How doest thou know what sort of king I have within me as companion?" He died at Konya on 17 December 1273. The diverse community of the city mourned him, and local Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> joined the crowd as his body was carried through the streets. He was buried beside his father, and a shrine, the "Green Tomb", whose building his close friend the Georgian princess and Seljuq queen Tamar Gurju Khatun sponsored, was raised over the grave.</p><p>His followers founded the Mevlevi Order after his death. Its first head was Hussam Chalabi; when he died in 1284, Rumi's only surviving son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Walad, who died in 1312, was installed as grand master, and it was he who organised the order. Its leadership has remained in Rumi's family at Konya without interruption since.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Mevlevi, known as the Whirling Dervishes, perform their dhikr as sama, to music set to poems from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masnavi">Masnavi</a> and the Divan. The order was well established in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. On 13 December 1925 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal Atatürk</a> passed a law closing all dervish lodges, dissolving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> orders and banning their ceremonies. In 1927 Rumi's mausoleum at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> was allowed to reopen as a museum, and in the 1950s the government began to let the dervishes perform once a year in Konya. In 2005 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> proclaimed the Mevlevi Sama Ceremony a Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity.</p><p>Many Muslims, particularly in the Turko-Persian cultural sphere, regard the Masnavi as one of the most important works of Islamic literature, and many commentators have called it the greatest mystical poem in world literature. Rumi's poetry influenced Persian literature and the literary traditions of Ottoman Turkish, Chagatai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashto">Pashto</a>, Kurdish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> and Bengali. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> his verse is displayed on city walls, sung in Persian music and read in school books, and it forms the basis of much classical Iranian and Afghan music.</p><p>His shrine remains a place of pilgrimage. His works have been translated into many languages; the English interpretations by Coleman Barks have sold more than half a million copies, and he has been described as the best-selling poet in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. UNESCO was associated in 2007 with the eight-hundredth anniversary of his birth, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> marked with a televised sama.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Rumi, born in 1207 in what is now Tajikistan, settled with his family at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and became a jurist and preacher. His bond with the dervish Shams-e Tabrizi made him a poet; he wrote the Divan and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masnavi">Masnavi</a> in Persian. After his death in 1273 his followers founded the Mevlevi Order.</em></p>

